@node Overview
@chapter Overview

@cindex overview
@cindex id Tech 1
A memory-safe Rust port of Doom 1, Doom II, and Ultimate Doom: one
codebase, one WAD-selectable engine, no C left in the tree.  id Tech 1
is the classic systems-porting benchmark --- a tight, well-studied
~55,000-line C engine (45k @file{.c} + 9.5k headers in
@code{linuxdoom-1.10}) with a software renderer, fixed-point math,
and a fully deterministic game loop --- which makes it the ideal
proving ground for Rust's safety guarantees under real-time
constraints.  No existing Rust effort has shipped a complete,
faithful port of all three IWADs; that gap is the opportunity.

@node Source Material
@chapter Source Material

@cindex source release
@cindex GPL
@cindex linuxdoom
id Software released the Doom source on 1997-12-23 as
@code{linuxdoom-1.10} --- the Linux port, because the DOS version's
licensed DMX sound library could not be published; the sound code was
replaced.  John Carmack authorized GPL relicensing on 1999-10-03, and
GPL-2.0 was formally applied to the canonical
@uref{https://github.com/id-Software/DOOM, id-Software/DOOM}
repository in January 2024.  Note that Bernd Kreimeier's pre-release
cleanup introduced a few bugs (later fixed in Doom Classic), so
vanilla-fidelity work should diff against known corrections.  Doom II
and Ultimate Doom run on the same engine; supporting all three is a
matter of IWAD detection, episode logic, and Doom II's cast/finale
screens.

@node Prior Art
@chapter Prior Art

@cindex prior art
@cindex room4doom
@cindex iron-doom
@cindex rust-doom
Three Rust efforts define the landscape; none is complete.

@table @code
@item room4doom
(MIT) The furthest along: playable, demo-compatible with vanilla Doom,
software renderer plus an optional 3D mode, OPL2 music emulation.
Hand-transliterated from the C, organized as per-system crates.  Still
missing the Doom II cast sequence, automap, and some audio effects;
development is sporadic.

@item iron-doom
(GPL-3.0) A Chocolate-Doom-based rewrite on Bevy's ECS.  Early stage
(~12 commits) and largely inactive --- an architecture experiment, not
a port.

@item rust-doom
(Apache-2.0, ~2.4k stars) cristicbz's renderer built from the
Doom specs rather than the C source.  No gameplay; inactive.  Useful
as a reference for idiomatic WAD/BSP handling in Rust.
@end table

@node Porting Strategies
@chapter Porting Strategies

@cindex porting strategy
@cindex doomgeneric
@cindex c2rust
@cindex transliteration
@table @asis
@item 1. Shim first, rewrite inward
@code{doomgeneric} (GPL-2.0) reduces the platform surface to six
functions: @code{DG_Init}, @code{DG_DrawFrame}, @code{DG_SleepMs},
@code{DG_GetTicksMs}, @code{DG_GetKey}, @code{DG_SetWindowTitle}.
Wrap them in Rust, get a running game on day one, then replace C
subsystems incrementally.  Fastest to playable; slowest to 100% Rust.

@item 2. Mechanical transpile, then refactor
@code{c2rust} emits working-but-@code{unsafe} Rust from the C tree;
the port then becomes a measurable campaign of driving the
@code{unsafe} count to zero, module by module.  Verifiable at every
step, but the intermediate code is not idiomatic.

@item 3. Manual transliteration
The room4doom route: rewrite the C by hand, preserving game-logic
structure (and thus demo compatibility) while making the renderer,
I/O, and platform layers idiomatic Rust from the start.  Highest
effort, cleanest result --- and the one that showcases memory-safe
design rather than inheriting C's shape.
@end table

@node Constraints
@chapter Constraints and Ecosystem

@cindex demo compatibility
@cindex fixed-point
@cindex IWAD licensing
@cindex crates
Determinism is the fidelity contract: demo playback requires bit-exact
16.16 fixed-point arithmetic and the original 256-entry
@code{P_Random} table --- no @code{f32} in game logic.  The engine's
pervasive global mutable state is the central Rust design problem;
plan ownership boundaries (a top-level game-state struct passed by
@code{&mut}, or per-system crates) before writing code.  Licensing:
the GPL covers only the code --- @file{DOOM.WAD} and @file{DOOM2.WAD}
remain commercial, so develop and CI against Freedoom's free IWADs and
the shareware @file{doom1.wad}.  Ecosystem: @code{winit} +
@code{pixels}/@code{softbuffer} (or SDL2 bindings) for video,
@code{rodio}/@code{cpal} for audio keep the stack memory-safe end to
end.

